# .geminirc_memory — SKELETON. Cross-cutting state and standing feedback.
# NOT bash. Do not source. Read it.
#
# Holds ONLY material with no single home directory: standing feedback, git
# hygiene, decisions that span repos. Anything scoped to one repo belongs in
# .geminirc_context_1 under that repo's own heading instead — keeping each
# directory's context self-contained is the whole point of the split.
#
# Format: dated entries, most recent state first per topic. Prune stale
# entries rather than letting them rot. Be explicit about what is CONFIRMED
# vs HYPOTHESIS vs FAILED.

=========================================================================
STANDING RULES  (do not delete — these are not observations, they are law)
=========================================================================

The four Prime Directives are in ContextPlease/README.md section 1.
Summarised so they cannot be missed:

  1. No renormalization, no fitted parameters, EVER. Report disagreement
     rather than closing a gap. If you cannot say where a number came
     from, you fitted it.
  2. All failures stay in code and data — with their diagnosis. They are
     how boundaries get explored. Do not tidy them away.
  3. Bash first.
  4. Always display /storage/emulated/0/ThePlace. Never /mnt/sdcard/.

=========================================================================
GIT / CREDENTIALS — handle with care
=========================================================================

Several repo `origin` remotes under $THEPLACE have GitHub PATs embedded in
plaintext (ghp_... visible via `git remote -v`). NEVER copy a remote URL
verbatim into a file that gets committed or pushed — always use the clean
https://github.com/... form. Flagged 2026-07-07; not known to be rotated.

=========================================================================
FEEDBACK LOG — how Cody wants this worked
=========================================================================

- Prefer `tree`, scoped and filtered, over brute-force `find`. Find-
  everything is slow and noisy.
- When Cody uses vivid or absolute language ("EXACT", "it's working"),
  treat it as describing a SPECIFIC case precisely — not as licence to
  generalise that property to adjacent tests.
- Do not reconstruct a mechanism from a verbal description repeatedly when
  it keeps failing. Go find and run the original implementation instead.
- Python first for all testing. C (ptol.c, monad.c) only after a Python
  result justifies it, or when C-level testing is the actual point.
- Context purity matters more than coverage. Do not load everything.
  Cody paces work deliberately; overloading a session has caused real
  problems before.
- For append-only logs, the END is what matters. Later entries supersede
  and frequently correct earlier ones.

